<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="android:editTextBackground">@drawable/rn_edit_text_material</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        THE LAUNCH SCREEN, AND WHY IT IS THE ACTIVITY'S THEME

        On Android the splash is not a screen the app draws. It is the window
        background the system puts up BEFORE any of our code runs, which is
        why it is declared as a theme in the manifest rather than rendered.
        That is also why it cannot be got wrong quietly: if this theme is
        missing, the first thing the player sees is a white rectangle.

        `postBootSplashTheme` is what the activity becomes once React Native
        has something to show. Without it the app keeps the splash theme for
        the rest of the session and AppTheme never applies.

        Android 12 took the splash over with its own windowSplashScreen API,
        and nothing here has to know: the library ships a values-v31 override
        of Theme.BootSplash that maps these same two attributes onto it. So
        inheriting is the whole of the Android 12 support, and writing a v31
        theme in this file would be a second copy of a decision the library
        already made.
    -->
    <style name="BootTheme" parent="Theme.BootSplash">
        <item name="bootSplashBackground">@color/bootsplash_background</item>
        <item name="bootSplashLogo">@drawable/bootsplash_logo</item>
        <item name="postBootSplashTheme">@style/AppTheme</item>
    </style>

</resources>
